  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather is expected to continue through this \nSunday with a day to day warming trend.\n\nStrong upper level ridging continues across the eastern CONUS with \nclear skies and above normal temperatures across much of the area. A \nsurface low will continue to progress eastward across northern \nOntario into Quebec that will bring breezy conditions late this \nafternoon and evening, but not much temperature change behind a weak \ncold front. Temperature Thursday afternoon have reached into the low-\n60s for spots across western NY while Friday afternoon highs will be \nonly a few degrees cooler. As high pressure shifts over the \nNortheast US and coastal areas this weekend, winds will turn more \nsoutherly limiting any cooling influence from the lakes, and \ndownsloping flow in combination with 850mb temps above the \nclimatological 90th percentile for mid-November. This will support \nSaturday afternoon highs 15-20 degF above average and warm weather \ncontinuing into Sunday ahead of a pattern shift beginning Sunday \nnight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Next chance for precipitation and cooler air arrive \nlate this weekend into early next week.\n\nIn the mid/upper-level atmosphere, a trough should overspread our \nregion from the north-central United States Sunday night and persist \nover and near the eastern Great Lakes through Tuesday. Ridging \nshould then build from the west Tuesday night through Wednesday. At \nthe surface, a cold front should sweep eastward through Western and \nCentral NY Sunday night and be followed by a ridge associated with a \ncolder air mass and building gradually from the northern Great \nPlains, northern Great Lakes, and vicinity through Wednesday. Daily \nhigh temperatures are forecast to reach the lower 30's to near 40F \n(around 5F to 10F below average) on Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nPeriodic rain showers are expected Sunday night into Monday morning \ndue to low-level convergence/moist ascent along the cold front and \nmoist isentropic ascent preceding the trough axis aloft. Primarily \ndry weather is then expected for the remainder of Monday through \nWednesday as stabilizing subsidence accompanies the ridge. However, \nthe post-front air mass should be cold enough and may be moist \nenough to support lake-effect rain and/or snow showers generally \nsoutheast and south of the eastern Great Lakes Monday into Tuesday. \nAny lake-effect precip should end by Wednesday as a lowering \nsubsidence inversion and continued low-level dry air advection cause \nlake-induced instability to wane.",
